# Line endings are content here, not a local preference.
#
# This repository has core.autocrlf=true and, until recently, no rules. That
# combination means `git checkout` on Windows rewrites LF to CRLF in the working
# tree while the committed blob stays LF -- so a file's bytes on disk differ from
# its bytes in history, and `git status` and `git diff` both stay silent about it
# because they compare normalised content.
#
# Two ways that has already cost time.
#
# Migrations are hashed. scripts/migrate.mjs records sha256 of each migration's
# content and refuses to run against a database where a recorded file no longer
# hashes the same -- correctly, because an edited migration means the schema and
# the repository disagree and no later migration reconciles that. A checkout that
# silently converted 0050_survey_participation.sql changed its hash, and
# migrate.mjs would have refused to apply anything against production, reporting
# that a file nobody had edited "has changed since it was applied".
#
# Patches miss. Anything that matches on an LF-anchored string -- a script, a
# codemod, a careful sed -- matches nothing in a CRLF working tree and reports
# success for having changed nothing.
#
# So: one rule for every text file, pinning both the committed blob and the
# checkout to LF.
#
# This changes no committed byte. Every blob in this repository is already LF
# (521 of them, verified with `git ls-files --eol`); what it fixes is the working
# tree, and it stops the drift coming back on the next clone.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never sniffs them, never converts them, and never tries to
# diff or merge them as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.ico   binary
*.bmp   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.dump  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.docx  binary

# Markup that happens to describe pictures is still text, and is diffed.
*.svg   text eol=lf

# Migrations, named explicitly. Redundant under the rule above, and kept because
# this is the file somebody edits when they want to relax something -- and the
# line they would have to delete on purpose is the reminder of why not to.
*.sql   text eol=lf
